<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jomo Kenyatta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1897–<a href="/years/1978">1978</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jomo Kenyatta was a Kikuyu nationalist leader imprisoned by the British during the Mau Mau emergency. As leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya-african-national-union">Kenya African National Union</a> he became prime minister at independence on 12 December 1963 and president when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> became a republic in 1964. He ruled until his death in 1978, making Kenya effectively a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta.jpg" alt="Jomo Kenyatta" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pridan Moshe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jomo_Kenyatta_(cropped)_in_June_15th,_1966.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kenyatta was born with the name Kamau in the rural village of Ngenda, to Kikuyu farmers named Mūigai and Wambūi. The Kikuyu kept no birth records, so his date of birth is not known; one biographer suggested 1890 or 1891, while a fuller analysis suggested about 1897 or 1898. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he lived among the Maasai, adopting a beaded belt called kĩnyata, from which he took his name.</p><p>He joined the Kikuyu Central Association. In February 1929 he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to press the Kikuyu land question. He returned to Britain in 1931 and did not go back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> for fifteen years, studying phonetics at University College London and social anthropology under Bronisław Malinowski at the London School of Economics.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Influenced by his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-padmore">George Padmore</a>, Kenyatta embraced Pan-African ideas and co-organised the 1945 Pan-African Congress in Manchester. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> in September 1946, farmed an estate at Gatundu and became vice-principal of the Koinange Independent Teachers' College. In 1947 he was elected President of the Kenya African Union, which lobbied for independence and drew wide African support but the animosity of white settlers. In 1952 he was among the Kapenguria Six, arrested and charged with masterminding the Mau Mau Uprising. He protested his innocence, a view later historians share, but was convicted. He was held at Lokitaung until 1959, then exiled to Lodwar until 1961.</p><p>After his release Kenyatta joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya-african-national-union">Kenya African National Union</a> in October 1961 and accepted its presidency. KANU won 83 of 124 seats in the May 1963 general election, and on 1 June 1963 he was sworn in as prime minister of the autonomous Kenyan government. In December 1964 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> was proclaimed a republic with Kenyatta as executive president. KANU's only rival, Oginga Odinga's Kenya People's Union, was prohibited from competing in elections.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1975, in the historian Maloba's words, he had in effect ceased to actively govern. In March 1975 the backbencher Josiah Mwangi Kariuki was kidnapped, tortured and murdered, after which support for the government eroded. Kenyatta died on 22 August 1978 and was succeeded by Daniel arap Moi.</p><p>He was called Mzee, meaning grand old man, and Father of the Nation, and a cult of personality surrounded him. His government pursued capitalist policies, expanded education and health care. Critics called his rule dictatorial and neocolonial, accused him of favouring the Kikuyu and of enabling corruption, and noted that Kikuyu still held most important government positions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jomo Kenyatta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its delegates, among them <b>Jomo Kenyatta</b>, Hastings Banda and Obafemi Awolowo, and representatives of African trade unions and student groups demanded independence for Africa.</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>A colonial court in Kenya sentenced <b>Jomo Kenyatta</b> and five others to seven years' hard labour for allegedly managing Mau Mau.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-8">8 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jomo Kenyatta</b>, jailed by the colonial authorities during the Mau Mau emergency, became prime minister and later the country's first president.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-12">12 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Kenya Colony became independent on 12 December 1963 under <b>Jomo Kenyatta</b>, leader of the Kenya African National Union (KANU), after the Mau Mau emergency and negotiations at Lancaster House.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenya">Kenya</a></li><li><blockquote>Kenya became independent on 12 December 1963 under <b>Jomo Kenyatta</b>, who had been imprisoned during the emergency.</blockquote><a href="/state/kenya-colony">Kenya Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gatundu, in what is now <a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a>. Died in Mombasa, in what is now <a href="/countries/kenya">Kenya</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-1.05,36.92&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Gatundu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5575 2203 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jomo Kenyatta"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6025 2473h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6025 2473h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-1.05,36.92&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
